   I see you.
   Mr. Rodríguez stood at the east door with a clipboard and a radio
and a thermos, wearing a bright yellow vest that said COMMUNITY
OBSERVER. A board member had given him the radio, and he held it
like a sacred object.
   In the corridor behind the reception area, I passed the green room.
   The door was open. An attendant in a gray polo stood beside it. In‐
side, a small, very beautiful room: soft light, a velvet chair, a tray of
fruit, a speaker the size of a paperback. Acoustic panels on every wall,
ceiling to floor. I stopped in the doorway before I knew I’d stopped.
   Nothing in that room made a sound.
   I’ve told you about the Early Calm classrooms, the ceiling baffles,
the noise floor of thirty decibels. This was the finished version, a room
where the pull I’d been fighting would fall on a person who couldn’t
push back. It would be easy. I could feel it opening a hand toward me,
patiently, with its palm up.
   “Would you like a moment, Mr. Cabral?” said the attendant.
   “No, gracias.”
   I said it to the room, and I walked away.



